From 7b083f631e08f512cfadff786523b0c7299fcada Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 18:17:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] riscv: honor _Float128 (IrType::U128 carrier) across the
 ABI
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

_Float128 lowers to the U128 carrier with is_f128_sse flagged on both
call sides, but RISC-V fell through to the x86 F128SseReg classification
and dropped the sign-bit intrinsics entirely:

  * classify_args_core: new f128_in_gp_pairs arm (RISC-V only) assigns
    an even-aligned GP register pair exactly like long double, with
    F128Stack overflow. The x86 F128SseReg branch is now unreachable for
    gp-pair targets; x86 classification is untouched (suite byte-checks
    confirm identical x86-64 f128 asm).

  * U128 loads record their destination as a full-precision f128 source
    and U128 stores propagate tracking to slot-anchored destinations
    (Direct/OverAligned only — Indirect/Reg homes do not anchor the
    bytes), mirroring the aarch64 fix. Without it, F128Neg staging on a
    freshly loaded carrier degraded to __extenddftf2(low qword) = -0.0.

  * operand_to_t0_t1 prefers the f128 tracker (direct and indirect
    homes) and stages IrConst::LongDouble carrier constants bit-exactly;
    call pre-convert staging loads U128-typed arguments directly from
    their slots and IrConst::I128 carrier constants stage their bits.

  * the intrinsic dispatcher gains the five sign-bit arms (F128Neg,
    LDFabs|F128Fabs, LDCopysign|F128Copysign) as pure high-qword sign
    edits on the a0:a1 pair — previously F128Neg hit the silent
    "x86-only SIMD no-op" catch-all and emitted nothing. Results store
    through the frame pointer (s0); the sp-relative form was only valid
    under call-staging adjustments.

Validation: RISC-V f1/f5/f6/f7 probes at GCC-oracle parity, f128_softfloat
rc=0 (was 1), f128_global_carrier rc=0; lib 1273/0/6; ARM 373/33/75 and
x86 536/5 unchanged; x86-64 f128 passthru asm byte-identical.
